    directAnswer: "Jet blast is the high-velocity, high-temperature exhaust plume behind operating aircraft engines. FAA AC 150/5300-13B gives design blast velocities by aircraft group — exceeding 100 mph well behind large jets — and any building, blast fence, sign, or equipment in the blast zone must be engineered for those velocity pressures plus heat and debris impact. Engineers map the blast contours from the aircraft mix, then design affected structures for the resulting loads or relocate them outside the contour.",
    answer: "Every jet engine is a wind machine pointed backward, and at takeoff power the exhaust velocities are extraordinary — AC 150/5300-13B documents blast velocities by aircraft design group, with wide-body exhaust exceeding hurricane-force winds dozens of feet behind the aircraft and remaining significant hundreds of feet back. Buildings in the blast zone face three loads: velocity pressure on walls, roofs, and cladding (computed like wind but often higher and more sustained); heat, which degrades asphalt, sealants, and some cladding systems; and debris — the blast picks up loose aggregate and turns it into projectiles. The engineering response starts with geometry: blast-contour mapping from the airport's aircraft mix and operating procedures shows which structures are exposed. Inside the contour, the options are hardening (structural design for blast pressures, heat-resistant materials, debris screens on air intakes and glazing protection) or relocation outside the contour. Blast fences — angled deflector walls — are themselves engineered structures with foundations designed for the overturning loads they intercept. Deicing pads, runup areas, cargo ramps, and maintenance aprons are the typical exposure zones; terminals are usually sited clear, but their jet bridges, service roads, and ground equipment are not. Apex Grid evaluates jet-blast exposure for airport buildings, designs hardening where the program requires proximity, and engineers blast fences and their foundations — with the analysis documented for FAA and AHJ review.",
