    answer: "An ARFF station is a fire station where seconds are the design criterion. FAA Part 139 sets the response-time requirements for certificated airports — typically requiring the first ARFF vehicle to reach the midpoint of the farthest runway within about three minutes of the alarm — and the station's site and building geometry exist to make that time. That drives everything: the station sits as close to the runway system as security and blast allow; apparatus bays are drive-through (enter from the back, exit directly toward the airfield) so trucks never reverse; turnout paths minimize turns and gates; and the alerting system wakes the crew and opens bay doors simultaneously. The building program includes apparatus bays sized for ARFF vehicles (much larger than municipal engines, with high doors and deep bays), crew living quarters for 24/7 staffing (bunk rooms, kitchen, day room, fitness), equipment and foam storage, decontamination, training rooms, and often the airport's emergency operations center. FAA AC 150/5210-15A (ARFF station planning) gives the programming guidance. Structurally, the bays need long clear spans with high doors and slab design for 50,000+ pound vehicles; MEP includes diesel exhaust capture in the bays, emergency power (NFPA 110), and the alerting/communications backbone. Many ARFF stations are AIP-eligible, which adds Buy American, DBE, and AC 150/5370-10H spec compliance. Apex Grid designs ARFF stations as complete packages — site geometry for response time, structural, MEP, and AIP compliance — stamped by licensed engineers.",
