    title: "What Drives Airport Terminal Electrical Design?" ,
    description: "Terminal electrical design centers on redundancy, selective coordination, and airfield interfaces — engineered so the building never goes dark.",
    h1: "What Drives Airport Terminal Electrical Design?",
    topic: "Aviation & Transportation Engineering",
    serviceHref: "/aviation-design/",
    directAnswer: "Airport terminal electrical design is driven by three requirements: redundancy (double-ended substations with tie breakers so maintenance never darkens the concourse), selective coordination (a fault in a concessions panel can't cascade to life-safety systems), and airfield interfaces (airfield lighting vaults, FAA equipment power, and emergency systems per NFPA 70 and NFPA 110). The design segregates life-safety, critical, and equipment branches with separately derived systems, backed by emergency generators sized for the full life-safety and operations load.",
    answer: "A terminal's electrical system is really three systems sharing a building: the life-safety system (egress lighting, fire alarm, smoke control, mass notification), the critical operations system (checkpoint equipment, baggage handling, communications, airport operations center), and the general building system (HVAC, lighting, concessions). The engineering keeps them rigorously separated — separately derived sources, segregated distribution, and selective coordination studies proving that a fault anywhere can't cascade into the life-safety or critical branches. Redundancy is architectural: double-ended unit substations with tie breakers let either utility feed or either transformer carry the building, so maintenance happens without outages. Emergency power follows NFPA 110 with generators sized for the full emergency load (not just code-minimum), automatic transfer schemes tested under load, and fuel storage for the required runtime. The terminal also interfaces with airfield systems: airfield lighting vaults (often fed from the terminal's distribution), FAA-owned equipment requiring utility-grade reliability, and emergency responder radio coverage (ERRCS/DAS) that needs dedicated power and pathways. Lighting design follows IES recommendations for the high-bay concourse with daylight harvesting at curtain walls and apron-floodlighting coordination outside. In renovation work, the phasing challenge dominates — cutovers sequenced so the operating terminal never loses life-safety power. Apex Grid's licensed electrical engineers design terminal power distribution, emergency systems, lighting, and airfield interfaces as one coordinated, stamped package — with the coordination studies and arc-flash analysis the AHJ expects.",
