    answer: "An airport's emergency power system is really four systems: the building emergency power (terminals, towers, ARFF stations) under NFPA 110; the UPS layer bridging the generator-start gap for anything that can't tolerate 10 seconds of darkness — radar data, communications, security systems, checkpoint equipment; the airfield lighting vaults with constant-current regulators keeping runway and taxiway lighting alive; and the fuel-farm and utility emergency loads. NFPA 110 classifies the systems by reliability: Level 1 (failure could cause loss of life) applies to towers, ARFF stations, and terminal life-safety; the generator, transfer switch, and fuel system are designed, installed, and tested as a system — not as separate equipment purchases. Sizing starts from the emergency load calculation but airports add the operations loads that can't stop: a terminal generator that powers egress lighting but not the checkpoint or the baggage system has missed the point. Fuel storage is the long pole — 48 to 72 hours of on-site fuel is typical for terminals, with the structural, fire-protection, and environmental design for the storage built into the project. The airfield side has its own architecture: lighting vaults with regulators per FAA AC 150/5340-30J, often with their own standby generation, because a dark runway closes the airport. Selective coordination ties it together — the studies proving faults can't cascade across the emergency distribution. Apex Grid's licensed electrical engineers design complete airport emergency power systems — generators, UPS, transfer schemes, vault interfaces, fuel storage, and the coordination studies — as stamped construction documents.",
