    title: "How Are Large Aircraft Hangar Doors Engineered?" ,
    description: "Hangar doors — bottom-rolling, vertical-lift, or fabric — drive the structural scheme more than any other decision. Here is the engineering.",
    h1: "How Are Large Aircraft Hangar Doors Engineered?",
    topic: "Aviation & Transportation Engineering",
    serviceHref: "/aviation-design/",
    directAnswer: "Large hangar doors are engineered as a vendor-structure partnership: the door manufacturer designs the leaf and operating equipment while the structural engineer designs everything it attaches to — rail foundations for bottom-rolling doors (settlement-free, or the door jams), header steel and headroom for vertical-lift doors, and the lateral scheme for fabric doors. Door reactions (often hundreds of kips) go into the structural model, and the door selection is locked before structural design advances because late changes cascade through the frame.",
    answer: "The hangar door is the most expensive single component in most hangars and the least forgiving of error — and it drives the structural scheme more than any other decision. Three systems dominate. Bottom-rolling doors — the standard for large hangars — roll on rails that demand continuous, settlement-free foundations; differential settlement as small as an inch across a 200-foot door can jam it, which pushes the geotechnical investigation and rail-foundation design harder than the frame itself in poor soils. The door leaves impose enormous lateral and vertical reactions on the end-wall columns, and the leaves themselves need wind design for the open and partially-open positions. Vertical-lift (megadoor-style) doors hang the entire leaf from the header: the header steel carries the full dead load of a leaf that can weigh dozens of tons, the headroom must be protected in the architectural section, and the lifting straps or screw jacks need power and controls integration. Fabric doors are lighter and faster but change the lateral-force path — the fabric can't brace the end wall, so the frame carries what a rigid door would have shared. In every case, the manufacturer engineers the leaf, tracks, and operators to their tested data, and the structural engineer designs the support steel, foundations, and building interfaces from the manufacturer's certified reactions. Wind design covers the door in every operating position — a half-open 200-foot door is a sail. Apex Grid locks the door selection with the owner before structural design advances, engineers the support steel and rail foundations from certified reactions, and coordinates the door, structural, and MEP (power, controls, safety interlocks) in one package — because the hangar projects that fail are the ones that redesign the frame around a late door change.",
